{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1461016-reworking-werewolf-regeneration","id":1461016,"title":"Reworking Werewolf Regeneration","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-09-23T06:59:00-07:00","id":1461016,"title":"Reworking Werewolf Regeneration","content":"I'm looking at tweaking a few aspects of Werewolves in 2e based on several years of play experience.<br /> <br /> One of the first areas I want to focus on is their regeneration. Werewolf regeneration got a fairly hefty boost in second edition, with the amount of bashing healed per turn now scaling with Primal Urge (instead of fixed at one), the ability to heal an equal amount of lethal damage for one essence, and the incredible power of Gauru form to heal all lethal and bashing each turn.<br /> <br /> Although Werewolves are on paper much better at regeneration in 2e than in 1e, some of the 2e changes have counteracted this, and I think their regeneration needs to be tweaked to take these changes into account.<br /> <br /> <u><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Out of Combat</span></b></u><br /> <br /> There are several things here that I don't think should be changed. I think it is a good decision for the splat as a whole to not allow any kind of agg regeneration in combat. This keeps silver and other banes suitably scary for Werewolves.<br /> <br /> I also think the passive out-of-combat healing speeds for Werewolves do not need any serious adjustment. Healing one lethal every 15 minutes seems about right for a Werewolf who isn't doing anything extra to speed their healing up.<br /> <br /> <u><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">In Combat</span></b></u><br /> <br /> The biggest issue here is that Werewolves' passive regeneration only addresses bashing damage. In 1e, there were many sources of bashing damage both in and out of combat. Lethal damage was reserved for powerful abilities, and weapons were partly balanced based on whether they dealt bashing or lethal damage.<br /> <br /> In 2e though, lethal damage is much more common. All weapons now do lethal, as do many more abilities. In fact, I can't ever remember taking bashing damage in combat in 2e, despite playing in 2 chronicles over 5 years. With rare exceptions, such as characters with ballistics armour or supernatural abilities like Vampire's damage reduction, all combat damage in 2e is lethal.<br /> <br /> This makes Werewolves' natural regeneration (outside of Gauru form and spending essence, which I'll get to) rarely come up in combat, which doesn't seem right. Werewolves should be able to leverage their regeneration in combat to deal with lethal damage.<br /> <br /> The way Werewolf 2e addresses this is by giving Werewolves the ability to spend essence to heal and this is where I think 2e makes one of its biggest missteps. Werewolves can spend an essence, and instead of healing bashing damage that turn, they instead heal an equal amount of lethal damage. I'm pretty sure the idea here is to give Werewolves cheap healing - if the Werewolf normally regenerates 3 bashing per turn then they get 3 lethal healed for just 1 essence, which is very efficient compared to other splats.<br /> <br /> The issue though is that Werewolves can't exceed their natural healing rate. So while a Blood Potency 3 Vampire can heal up to 3 lethal per turn for a cost of 3 Vitae, a Primal Urge 3 Werewolf cannot heal 3 lethal in one turn, no matter how much essence they have. They would instead need to spend 3 essence over 3 turns, which is patently <i>worse</i> than the Vampire's healing ability. Werewolf regeneration is one of their iconic abilities and so they should at least be on par with most of the other splats.<br /> <br /> One of the reasons this doesn't work is that the way Werewolf healing scales with Primal Urge is unbalanced. Werewolves heal 1 bashing per turn at PU 1 which increases all the way to 6 bashing per turn at PU 10. However this doesn't scale linearly.<br /> <br /> I picked PU 3 in the example because I think this is where the scaling is at its worst. Werewolves only start healing 2 bashing per turn at PU 4 and 3 bashing per turn at PU 6. This makes the chart ridiculously top-heavy. A Werewolf at Primal Urge 5 probably has some of the worst in-combat healing of any splat at that power level.<br /> <br /> In general I don't think that good regeneration should be restricted to Werewolves at the higher levels of Primal Urge simply because I have never seen a player character exceed 5 in a power stat in any game, even in my Vampire game where the passing of centuries and occasional diablerie has given them free boosts to Blood Potency. This is especially true in Werewolf since in 5 years I never once saw a player buy a single dot in PU, much less 5+ dots.<br /> <br /> <u><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Conclusion</span></b></u><br /> <br /> The effect of the situation above is that players use Gauru form as the primary means of healing in Werewolf. This both cheapens the normal regeneration that Werewolves are supposed to always possess, and makes the killing form feel rather silly since it gets used in every combat not to deal the killing blow, but to recover after taking a face full of lead. Players will even do things like shift into Urshul first so that they can fill as many health boxes with lethal as possible before cashing in on the regeneration provided by Gauru form.<br /> <br /> <u><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Solutions</span></b></u><br /> <br /> There are a number of ways I have considered solving the issues above:<br /> <br /> <b>1. Fix regeneration scaling with Primal Urge</b><br /> <br /> This one is fairly straightforward. The obvious solution here is to make the amount of bashing healed per turn equal to Primal Urge, so at PU 4 a Werewolf would heal 4 bashing per turn, and for 1 essence they could heal 4 lethal instead. One of the nice things about this solution is that it makes Werewolves' per-turn healing ability keeps pace with Vampires while remaining much cheaper.<br /> <br /> However I'm not sure I like how much of a buff this is to Werewolf regeneration. At the low end it makes bashing damage in combat almost irrelevant, but I don't mind that too much since bashing is already irrelevant to Werewolves in most combats. However, even being able to heal 4 or 5 lethal for a single essence would make low PU Werewolves much tougher.<br /> <br /> It also makes high PU Werewolves nearly impossible to kill in any form without Silver. This concerns me a little bit more. Would high PU Werewolves eschew the Killing Form? It has numerous other bonuses so perhaps not. However I still don't like the idea that something like an Idigam would have a really hard time killing a Hishu form Werewolf if their Primal Urge was near the maximum.<br /> <br /> If I was going to implement this, I think healing 10 bashing damage per turn at PU 10 is a little silly. Instead, I think I would simply make PU 10 Werewolves heal <i>all</i> bashing every turn, which would mean for 1 essence they could heal <i>all</i> lethal, which would be a nice capstone.<br /> <br /> <b>2. Give Werewolves the ability to heal/downgrade lethal naturally without spending essence</b><br /> <br /> I don't like this since it would mean also buffing Werewolf healing out of combat, and I think out of combat Werewolf healing is exactly where it needs to be.<br /> <br /> <b>3. Give Werewolves damage reduction like Vampires</b><br /> <br /> This is nice because it lets Werewolves keep their regeneration as only applying to bashing, but I don't think it's appropriate for Werewolves. Werewolf already has merits and a few Gift Facets to make them tougher. The only splats that get this as a natural ability are Vampires and Mummies, and in both cases it's due to their undead nature - which Werewolves most certainly are not.<br /> <br /> <b>4. Replace the essence spend effect on Werewolf regeneration</b><br /> <br /> I think this is the solution I like best. Instead of spending one essence and healing an amount of lethal damage determined by their Primal Urge, the Werewolf would spend one essence to downgrade <i>all</i> lethal damage to bashing.<br /> <br /> This is nice for several reasons. One is that it is a very-cost effective way to heal. I don't think any other splat has a natural healing ability that is this cheap.<br /> <br /> The second is that it is not much of a change to the power level of Werewolves. It ends up being a bit of a buff at the low end and a slight nerf at the high end. It also preserves the value of Gauru form since it becomes the only way to instantly heal a large amount of lethal.<br /> <br /> The third is that is preserves the value and the flavour of the Werewolf's natural per-turn bashing regeneration. Now you first downgrade lethal to bashing by spending essence, and then your natural regeneration heals it over a few turns. I would probably also tweak the per-turn regeneration of werewolves here, so that the amount healed per turn goes up with every second dot of PU (i.e. 2 of PU 3, 3 at PU 5 etc.).<br /> <br /> I'm also fond of splats having unique ways of accomplishing the same goal. Most splats can spend their essence equivalent to heal, but none of them do it quite like this.<br /> <br /> I'd be very interested to hear if anyone has thoughts on the above or has come up with alternative solutions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2384,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","user":"Aurumae","date":". 09-23-2021, 10:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-23T10:15:19-07:00","id":1461045,"title":"","content":"I feel like before I can warrant any acceleration of regeneration, I kind of have to ask questions about how the game is being played, because even for as violent a game as Werewolf can be...it seems like there's a weird approach to violence at work here that might be skewing with the effects.<br /> <br /> First off, the three tiers of damage are here for an escalation of tension, and the mention of rarely coming across bashing tells me that the two chronicles are much more bloodthirsty than the average game. Violence usually comes about as failure of all other solutions to a scene, or as a expedient solution for those who can adroitly use it to their benefit (ie, is it going to be easier to get in by punch out the guards versus sneaking past or lying your way past them somehow? for some, it is.) and most people are less interested in murder and more interested in just getting someone out of the way. Lethal comes by and large as a deterrent or as a way of forcing choices (do you go after someone or stop your comrade from bleeding out, as a basic one), usually to facilitate one side getting to do that thing they want to do*. Aggravated by and large is there when either murder is the point, or both sides are playing with absolute stakes, really and truly &quot;Do or die&quot; situations<br /> <br /> How splats heal alters the effect of this in regards to their themes and motifs to the effect of both the horror they portray and the horror they experience. In the case of werewolf, basic regeneration of bashing is there to basically get the pleasantries out of the way and emphasize the horror of the Uratha. For Lethal, that shifts a little-a werewolf can become even more at home with the violence of their lives <i>at the cost</i> of leaning harder into their spiritual aspects. a Werewolf who's constantly relying on quick healing lethal is going to spend more time at the Loci, in the Shadow hunting prey spirits, and otherwise doing things that drive them away from humanity, with the ultimate expression of that being pressuring the Uratha to waste their precious time of killing form and risk Rage. A werewolf can, of course, pump the breaks and let their still advanced healing do the work, and that may even appeal to their Bone Anchor, but has the consequences of messing with the hunt and pushing them away from their spiritual side.<br /> <br /> All of which is to say &quot;how much of the conflict is being played really build tension and express monstrosity, and how often is a moment of violence used to push the internal conflict of an Uratha trying to maintain Harmony?&quot;<br /> <br /> The second part that builds on the first one is that Werewolves are scapels, not swords. They tend towards skirmishes rather than outright tanky combat. In this light, while a werewolf might take a big hit, a lot of times, that large hit is not going to be so important that they can't hang back and heal up-again, as the long term hunt of harrying and wearing down a prey and their resources goes, the werewolves have the advantage-they can always pull back, wait a bit, and then come back to find the prey only having just caught their breath to have another go at dividing them up, tiring them out, cutting off their options.<br /> <br /> This means that in order to create those opportunities to force the conflict, the prey needs to create situations that force the Uratha to spend more than they normally would in a moment of conflict, make a situation where sticking around and facing the conflict of sliding spiritually and/or risking Rage is the better option that harrying over time. And so my question there is &quot;is that happening?&quot;<br /> <br /> If we assume that Forsaken is being run more like a traditional combat focused game, and the goal is to make it clear that the Forsaken are Apex Predators in <i>that</i> arena, then yeah, the hacks work fine-but it feels to me more like there's probably misconceptions on how Forsaken runs it's violence.<br /> <br /> EDITL as a general rule of thumb, it's best to assume that even supernatural baddies who can handle violence better than the average human opt to try and avoid prolonged direct conflict on account of <i>most</i> things dying when they are killed. Even if you can walk it off, it's better when you don't have to. Most things hew in that direction.<br /> <br /> *with the curve thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 09-23-2021, 03:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-23T10:56:39-07:00","id":1461058,"title":"","content":"It also feels weird to me that the conclusion is that werewolves innate healing isn't on par with other splats when the majority of CofD splats don't get innate regeneration. Even the vampire vs. werewolf issue is skewed by the fact that vampires have to spend blood to heal. If they can run away from a fight, they still have to spend blood to heal. The Uratha can run away and not spend any Essence to heal, and shift to Gauru and not have to spend any Essence to heal. Vampires (who also have a strong mythological and fictional pressure to be good self-healers) having a better power point to lethal healing rate as power stat goes up isn't the whole picture there.<br /> <br /> I do think that #4 is probably the best option of those listed if you still feel like buffing in-combat regen a bit, but I'm not sure how much it's really going to change larger dynamics. The game considers &quot;fight in Urshul and then shift to Gauru if you have to,&quot; how things are supposed to work, not a bug; for example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-23T11:14:17-07:00","id":1461060,"title":"","content":"Yeah, that's part of the harrying conversation I was putting down.<br /> <br /> A pack can hit a target, do what they can in a short amount of time, then hang back, and in the time they're back up to par, their target might've at best dealt with bashing damage or A lethal damage, and probably not any tilts OR spend resources that will almost invariably cause them to leave more secure locations to get out in the open where the Uratha can harry them again.<br /> <br /> This also tends to have the benefit of having a prey reveal what all their resources are and where the material ones are located, which can then be used against them and to the packs immediate and long term benefit.<br /> <br /> And in that view, that cost-free regen does <i>a lot</i> of work.<br /> <br /> EDIT: putting rates down for clarity:<br /> <br /> Baseline humans recover a bashing damage every 15 minutes and a lethal every two days. Werewolves heal bashing every turn(/3 seconds) and lethal according to the rate as determined by Primal Urge every 15 minutes. Most of the major splats heal like humans unless they spend resources.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Also, while i noticed the out of COmbat healing being fine, part of the point is that werewolves aren't inclined to linger in combat-they get in, wreck shop, and then get out, wait for a while, and then rinse and repeat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 09-23-2021, 03:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-23T12:26:52-07:00","id":1461070,"title":"","content":"So before I get into this, I think I should point out that my main interest in fixing this is mechanical and improving how things feel moment to moment in combat when it does happen. I notice that almost all of your arguments <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar99_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"99\" data-vbnamecard=\"99\">ArcaneArts</a> deal with the overall themes and feel of the game. I also feel like the current regeneration doesn't support those as well as it could, but we may be fundamentally at cross-purposes here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1461016-reworking-werewolf-regeneration?p=1461045#post1461045\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel like before I can warrant any acceleration of regeneration, I kind of have to ask questions about how the game is being played, because even for as violent a game as Werewolf can be...it seems like there's a weird approach to violence at work here that might be skewing with the effects.</div> </div> </div> </div>Normally I might agree with you, but this is Werewolf. You don't talk to the claimed, or the <i>Beshilu</i>, or the things that used to be normal Spirits until the Idigam showed up, you get your teeth into them. Werewolf is much more violent than Vampire in my experience, even when though my Vampire game is currently set during the 6th century. The frequency of violence against <i>Humans</i> is probably about the same, but where Vampire is a game about other Vampires and humans, Werewolf is a game about other Werewolves, humans, hosts, Spirits, Ridden/Claimed, the Idigam, and assorted other things that should not be. Most of those things aren't interested in talking, and to be fair neither are the Uratha who hunt them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1461016-reworking-werewolf-regeneration?p=1461045#post1461045\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">First off, the three tiers of damage are here for an escalation of tension, and the mention of rarely coming across bashing tells me that the two chronicles are much more bloodthirsty than the average game. Violence usually comes about as failure of all other solutions to a scene, or as a expedient solution for those who can adroitly use it to their benefit (ie, is it going to be easier to get in by punch out the guards versus sneaking past or lying your way past them somehow? for some, it is.) and most people are less interested in murder and more interested in just getting someone out of the way. Lethal comes by and large as a deterrent or as a way of forcing choices (do you go after someone or stop your comrade from bleeding out, as a basic one), usually to facilitate one side getting to do that thing they want to do*. Aggravated by and large is there when either murder is the point, or both sides are playing with absolute stakes, really and truly &quot;Do or die&quot; situations</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't doubt that this is the intent, but it's simply not how the system works in 2e. It's hard to actually deal bashing damage in 2e even if you wanted to. The only reliable way is by punching someone, but if you take a look at even the fighting style merits for mortals, most of those upgrade your fists into lethal weapons. Uratha deal lethal damage with all their natural weapons in all forms, with the exception that they don't have claws in Hishu.<br /> <br /> In your examples, I would probably use Snarl of the Predator to get past the guards, and since Werewolves can't bleed out I would almost certainly pursue the prey to stop it getting away. If it's a <i>Beshilu </i>who just tore open one of my packmates it's only going to come back bigger and nastier with friends.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1461016-reworking-werewolf-regeneration?p=1461045#post1461045\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How splats heal alters the effect of this in regards to their themes and motifs to the effect of both the horror they portray and the horror they experience. In the case of werewolf, basic regeneration of bashing is there to basically get the pleasantries out of the way and emphasize the horror of the Uratha. For Lethal, that shifts a little-a werewolf can become even more at home with the violence of their lives <i>at the cost</i> of leaning harder into their spiritual aspects. a Werewolf who's constantly relying on quick healing lethal is going to spend more time at the Loci, in the shadow hunting prey animals, and otherwise doing things that drive them away from humanity, with the ultimate expression of that being pressuring the Uratha to waste their precious time of killing form and risk Rage. A werewolf can, of course, pump the breaks and let their still advanced healing do the work, and that may even appeal to their Bone Anchor, but has the consequences of messing with the hunt and pushing them away from their spiritual side.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure that I follow this argument. A Werewolf who's in a fight where they might be calling on their essence-based healing is going to be spending a tonne of essence to fuel Gifts regardless. Saving essence isn't really a choice, and allowing yourself to take wrap-around agg damage doesn't help anyone.<br /> <br /> Or to put it another way, if you're in this situation you're probably going to be heading back to the locus at the first opportunity regardless of whether you choose to fuel your healing abilities or not. In our games it was usually the Uratha with a more spiritual focus who aided the rest of the pack with Gifts rather than the front line fighters who ran out of essence first.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1461016-reworking-werewolf-regeneration?p=1461045#post1461045\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All of which is to say &quot;how much of the conflict is being played really build tension and express monstrosity, and how often is a moment of violence used to push the internal conflict of an Uratha trying to maintain Harmony?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>A lot of the horror in Werewolf is due not to the Uratha themselves, but to the kind of body horror that the things they're hunting inspire. Unlike Vampires, Uratha themselves are only really the source of the horror in the story when they're hunting humans and I think it should be stressed that Uratha aren't always hunting humans. In fact in our chronicles we only hunted them when absolutely necessary, there was plenty of other more dangerous prey for us to concern ourselves with.<br /> <br /> Maintaining Harmony is a challenge when fighting Humans or other Werewolves, but hunting hosts, claimed, or <i>magath</i> doesn't really have an ethical dimension.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1461016-reworking-werewolf-regeneration?p=1461045#post1461045\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The second part that builds on the first one is that Werewolves are scapels, not swords. They tend towards skirmishes rather than outright tanky combat. In this light, while a werewolf might take a big hit, a lot of times, that large hit is not going to be so important that they can't hang back and heal up-again, as the long term hunt of harrying and wearing down a prey and their resources goes, the werewolves have the advantage-they can always pull back, wait a bit, and then come back to find the prey only having just caught their breath to have another go at dividing them up, tiring them out, cutting off their options.<br /> <br /> This means that in order to create those opportunities to force the conflict, the prey needs to create situations that force the Uratha to spend more than they normally would in a moment of conflict, make a situation where sticking around and facing the conflict of sliding spiritually and/or risking Rage is the better option that harrying over time. And so my question there is &quot;is that happening?&quot;<br /> <br /> If we assume that Forsaken is being run more like a traditional combat focused game, and the goal is to make it clear that the Forsaken are Apex Predators in <i>that</i> arena, then yeah, the hacks work fine-but it feels to me more like there's probably misconceptions on how Forsaken runs it's violence.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with you to a large extent. However I think that part of building interesting conflicts as a storyteller in Werewolf involves intentionally overwhelming the players a little bit. A pack of Werewolves can deal with almost any threat given sufficient time - as you've said they can flee, lick their wounds for a day, and be back at full strength again tomorrow. To maintain tension, the pack should always need to be doing several things at once, and having to run triage. This doesn't mean that they're always fighting, and I think that in most cases a hunt can end with no combat, even if the Werewolves choose to do violence. Usually simply catching their prey is the hard part, winning the fight is a foregone conclusion.<br /> <br /> But every once in a while there's going to be a big nest of <i>shartha</i> and the Werewolves are going to have to exterminate it. It's really in these situations that I think the current regeneration system doesn't work well. In all likelihood, none of the <i>shartha</i> are going to deal any bashing damage in the coming fight, and even if you hunted them perfectly and took advantage of every power at your disposal, things are still going to get very bloody. Simply put, when you're engaged in those scenes, Werewolves don't feel like they have much regeneration at all since none of the damage they're taking is bashing, and if you have experience playing other splats like Vampire or Geist, Werewolves at equivalent levels of Primal Urge feel like they are weirdly handicapped when it comes to healing themselves.<br /> <br /> I think the lure of the killing form is important and in my analysis I did try to account for this in my proposed solutions. It's really this feeling of being in an important fight and asking &quot;why is healing so hard? I'm a Werewolf!&quot; that I want to address.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2384,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","user":"Aurumae","date":". 09-23-2021, 03:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-23T14:45:45-07:00","id":1461091,"title":"","content":"It seems like you're not really going to find something satisfactory if you're trying to overwhelm your players... because if you boost the PCs you just boost what it means to be in a situation where they'll be overwhelmed. There isn't some default mechanics for, say, taking on a nest of shartha. So as the ST designing the encounter is going to account for the PCs' abilities, how much does changing regeneration really matter to the fun of playing out the fight? A well calibrated fight with the RAW vs a well calibrated fight with a house rule is going to really change what besides how strong the shartha are to deal with a change in how strong the PCs are?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-23T17:06:53-07:00","id":1461110,"title":"","content":"So I want to address this, but I'm still at work, and after work I'm probably grocery shopping, so dealing with the whole is probably gonna be broken up into bite sized pieces-which is not great for addressing things, I know, but this is the world we live in.<br /> <br /> I want to ground future discussion by keeping in mind the major motif of Forsaken in the form of <a href=\"https://truenaturefoundation.org/research/how-wolves-change-rivers/\" target=\"_blank\">wolves changing the rivers</a>, contextualized with <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/433128-siskur-dah-2ed-sacred-hunt-and-it-s-secrets?p=473087#post473087\">this post</a> from years ago.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1461016-reworking-werewolf-regeneration?p=1461070#post1461070\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Normally I might agree with you, but this is Werewolf. You don't talk to the claimed, or the <i>Beshilu</i>, or the things that used to be normal Spirits until the Idigam showed up, you get your teeth into them. Werewolf is much more violent than Vampire in my experience, even when though my Vampire game is currently set during the 6th century. The frequency of violence against <i>Humans</i> is probably about the same, but where Vampire is a game about other Vampires and humans, Werewolf is a game about other Werewolves, humans, hosts, Spirits, Ridden/Claimed, the Idigam, and assorted other things that should not be. Most of those things aren't interested in talking, and to be fair neither are the Uratha who hunt them.</div> </div> </div> </div>So I'mma probably blow your nerd mind by pointing out that* not only <i>can</i> you talk to Claimed or the Beshilu or Idigam-afflicted spirits, you <i>probably </i>want to, and this is <i>in fact</i> a big reason why the Elodoth are the way they are, and to a much lesser extent why the Cahalith and Ithauer are the way they are.<br /> <br /> THe first thing to keep in mind that for as often any given prey represents either a major source of imbalance in a territory, or is at least the major beneficiary <i>of</i> a major source of imbalance-for every one of those, there's usually prey who are up in your grill <i>because</i> of that imbalance. One spirit flees into the Flesh and becomes a Claimed because a shift in spirit courts made them likely prey, one swarm of shartha shows up because some Uratha or human's development project next door caused them to flee into your territory. These things might be amenable to getting back where it came from (or so help you) and backtracking to original states-if a spirit doesn't have to worry about being lunch and can instead go back to eating happily at a loci watering hole, then they don't need to Claim that human anymore, and if a shartha swarm can go back to their original den instead of being hunted down by an apex predator, it's not hard to see the cost/benefit ratio there. And for the werewolvesit generally means they get to keep ahold of any resources they might have wasted on an inferior problem while another problem just gets to build over them-and hell, it might let them get in as much of a first turn advantage against said potential future prey as possible and let them nip problems in the bud quicker than they might otherwise be. And this conversations open other conversations that might be beneficial.<br /> <br /> That neatly leads into the second thing-sure, the thing you are facing down is probably an unholy abomination of flesh and spirit, <i>but</i> it's also an entity that has power and influence to shape your territory in ways that may be beneficial to you. Sure, the Claimed in front of your snoze isn't pretty, but gosh darnit it, wouldn't you know that it has influence over a purview that you were looking to improve upon over in the shopping district. THe Azlu definitely aren't pretty, but hey, you know, the Gauntlet is a little weak in some of these areas, and it would also be a huge boon to reshaping that one spirit court if we deepened the Gauntlet in their seat of power. A lot of prey have the ability to contribute something to your territory, and if you can bargain for that rather than trying to harry them over to a place and corral them to force them to do that work, why the hell not?<br /> <br /> THird thing-so, okay, maybe not a victim of environmental shift, maybe not the best advantage for your territory. But you know what any given prey is? Another tool against your enemies, that's what. Sure, this Claimed's a butt, but the Pure next door suck more. Sure, you'll never get the Azlu out of your hair, but that one Spirit Court's really pressing their advantage. Given a choice between being hunted down and being a provisional aid in some other conflict, any given prey's likely to act on that deal because, you know-one of them is getting hunted down. It saves you resources, or augments them for really big problems.<br /> <br /> Now, does this mean you can just talk to them to resolve issues? No usaully, it'll involve some conflict to get into a talking mood for some of them. Does it mean the results are always pretty? No, there's plenty of backstabbing and outmaneuvering and flanking and what not. Hell, sometimes it's a trap. And sometimes, particularly in the case of Idiam-afflicted entities, there may not actually be the recourse of discourse. The thing of the matter, though, of being an Apex predator in a world where people die when they are killed and people would rather avoid that is, given the choice being killed until dead and working with other options.....a shockingly large number of things are gonna wanna talk rather than risk the hunt. For the Uratha, it often means getting at least some information they might not otherwise have and at most gives them vital ways to affect the rivers of spiritual resonance that define their territory that would otherwise take a shit ton of work to do. It also doesn't hurt that that reputation builds up over time, which means that for every prey that wants to cut the legs out from under a werewolf, there's gonna be those who know that working with the pack has benefits and payouts, which will result in more ready allies in times of need than not. Every prey is a resource, and <i>just</i> hunting them is wasting a lot of potential resource.<br /> <br /> Eventually, yes, some prey are going to be in a place beyond words or negotiation, where it's all just violence and the hunt between the twain-but poor is the werewolf who does not understand the ecosystem of their territory and turns those within it to their ends.<br /> <br /> Yes, Werewolf is a violent, bloody, predator-eat-prey game-but that violence isn't <i>arbitrary</i>, and not for nothing does Luna wear the face of the Envoy and Diplomat, and brands a fifth of her Children thusly as well.<br /> <br /> *Pikachu is definitely a boy in the anime, cause attract got used on him by a girl pokemon and Pokemon runs on heterosexuals only rules.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-23T18:17:30-07:00","id":1461117,"title":"","content":"In our first lookthrough I was noticing the scaling was a bit uneven, so in our first game after a bit we retooled it to be more evenly paced. I forget offhand but I believe it jumped to 2 Bashing at Primal Urge 2, then 3 at 4 and up one again every two Primal Urge. It was more to incentivize the raising of Primal Urge, as not getting that regen boost for 15 experiences can seem so far.<br /> <br /> While I like the idea of downgrading all Lethal to Bashing, it honestly seems more like it should be a Gift than an inherent ability. And just tying to that, while vampires are known for downgrading Lethal to Bashing, it's not fair to say that werewolves don't at all, they just have a higher bar and downgrade Aggravated to Lethal. Werewolf the Forsaken Second Edition p94, &quot;Any source of harm that would cause aggravated damage to a human, including massive bodily devastation, only causes lethal damage to Uratha. As such, most unwitting attackers must do enough lethal damage to cause it to \u201croll over\u201d into aggravated damage before it poses a real problem for werewolves.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-23T18:26:15-07:00","id":1461118,"title":"","content":"This looks like fertile ground for a new Wolf Gift, actually.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-23T19:25:44-07:00","id":1461120,"title":"","content":"With everything that ArcaneArts and Heavy Arms already said, I would only add that in your case it seems that what your players need isn't better regeneration, but to actually use the Gauru form.<br /> <br /> It is a last-resort form, sure. But that's what you're pressing them to. You said it yourself that you're intentionally overwhelming them. Gauru is there precisely to fight overwhelming odds. At this point it isn't a hunt anymore, or at least the PCs aren't the hunters, so why are they still holding it?<br /> <br /> To me it seems less that the rules don't work with the game's intention and more that you don't play the game as intended. Which isn't a problem, by the way. But then heed Heavy Arms' advice, think about what this game really is on your table and we may have better solutions for what you want.<br /> <br /> A new Gift may indeed be an excellent solution here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-23T21:54:40-07:00","id":1461140,"title":"","content":"To add to Arc and Arms's points, I'll just observe that this confusion has been had before over in Beast, where people didn't understand why the rapid-healing Atavism was in-affinity for the stalking-and-ambush Family: &quot;wolfpack tactics&quot; are guerrilla warfare. Werewolves aren't warriors, they're <i>hunters</i>, and that means regeneration is no excuse for not fighting smart. (Even if you're a big beefy brick of a Rahu, tactics serve you well.)<br /> <br /> Also, I get that Gifts are more mechanically unique and therefore draw more interest, but at the bottom of the Primal Urge ladder Merits are the more efficient purchase; Favored Form, Fortified Form, and Living Weapon are all free to use. Putting dots into your Totem only has drawbacks in the very long term, maybe. Werewolf Fighting Merits allow you to coordinate and make use of your Renown without necessarily burning Essence.<br /> <br /> Like Changeling, Werewolf doesn't set aside freebies or a special Experience type for raising your Supernatural Potency, but the Siskur-Dah and Ban Conditions from various sources are there to act as Beat engines for you to build up more stats than just your Renown.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-24T04:09:03-07:00","id":1461182,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2384_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2384\" data-vbnamecard=\"2384\">Aurumae</a>, first, I completely support you (or anyone else) changing what works for their game, so move forward with the changes if that's what works.<br /> <br /> With that said, the issue I see with the changes are that they're based on missing a fundamental point about Werewolf: The Forsaken. Despite how tough they appear, the Uratha aren't designed to be fighters. They're hunters. Forsaken aren't the Garou. They're not warriors for Gaia who hit hard and take damage and regenerate in moments with their Rage. Uratha are thinking, pack hunting predators. They avoid getting into drawn out fights where their lethal regeneration may be slightly less than the prey they're taking down. They harry the prey, work it into a corner, take away its advantages, then they strike when it's vulnerable. They use their forms to their advantage in wearing it down, and when they strike they do it hard with Gauru form and they kill quickly while absorbing damage for a very short time.<br /> <br /> Like other pack hunters, if they can't do that they act intelligently. If one is injured, that individual falls back and the pack carries on if it's feasible. If more are injured, or if the kill in no longer feasible, they all retreat and regroup. They take the time for 15 minutes per lethal to take effect. They heal up very quickly without any additional resource expenditure, and they continue to harry the foe while it's also trying to regroup. They don't give it time to rest and recouperate, they apply the pressure until they're ready to attack again.<br /> <br /> The Uratha's Gifts and powers revolve around this concept. They keep the prey off balance and weakened. The Uratha may need to fall back to heal but they're not letting that vampire (or whatever) take its leisure to drink some blood and regain its strength. They scare off its prey, they keep humans away, they bring Gifts to bear and if it does find a victim to drain they hit it while it's vulnerable because the loss of resource is worse for the vampire than it is for them.<br /> <br /> If your several years of play experience is finding the Uratha characters falling behind because they can't just stand in a drawn out fight and always go punch-for-punch with their foes, by all means make the changes. But I'd suggest that the players need to lean more into what makes Uratha werewolves, and stop trying to play Garou werewolves. (And I acknowledge that I make this statement without first hand knowledge of how your games are structured, based purely on the information you've shared.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","user":"Bunyip","date":".  Fixing typos 09-24-2021, 07:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-24T06:42:45-07:00","id":1461198,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bunyip</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1461016-reworking-werewolf-regeneration?p=1461182#post1461182\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If your several years of play experience is finding the Uratha characters falling behind because they can't just stand in a drawn out fight and always go punch-for-punch with their foes, by all means make the changes. But I'd suggest that the players need to lean more into what makes Uratha werewolves, and stop trying to play Garou werewolves. (And I acknowledge that I make this statement without first hand knowledge of how your games are structured, based purely on the information you've shared.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Since there has been a lot of emphasis has been on the &quot;werewolves are hunters and are actually conniving bastards like everything else in the Chronicles world&quot;, I did want to return and iterate on this point:<br /> <br /> If you want a more traditionally combative game, you can, and your hacks do work for that. You might have some fun returning to some of these points a a way of diversifying the gameplay for your group, but if your group enjoys a more &quot;stand and deliver, kill everything when you show up&quot; style of gameplay, yer doing okay with providing a version of Forsaken that does that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-24T07:48:21-07:00","id":1461215,"title":"","content":"For a more combative game I'd go for removing the sting of the Gauru form, or at least reducing it. This seems like a better option, instead of messing with the regeneration that will reduce the comparative benefits of the battle form (making it even less attractive), make the battle form more accessible.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-26T12:28:04-07:00","id":1461589,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the feedback everyone. Upon reflection I think I agree with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar886_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"886\" data-vbnamecard=\"886\">nofather</a> that this would work better as a Gift than as a change to the Uratha's normal regeneration. Based on that I've come up with a draft Gift of Regeneration Wolf Gift:<br /> <br /> <b>Gift of Regeneration</b><br /> <br /> <b>FEIGN WEAKNESS (CUNNING)</b><br /> The Uratha stifles her regeneration to lure her prey in for the killing blow.<br /> <b>Cost:</b> 1 Essence<br /> <b>Action:</b> Reflexive<br /> The Uratha sacrifices her regeneration to gain an equal number of bonus dice on her next attack. For example if she would heal 3 damage she instead gains 3 additional dice. This Facet cannot be used if the Uratha is at full health.<br /> <br /> <b>KNIT FLESH (GLORY)</b><br /> By focusing her regeneration the Uratha can speed the healing of grievous injuries.<br /> <b>Cost:</b> 1 Essence<br /> <b>Action:</b> Reflexive<br /> Upon activating this Facet the Uratha downgrades a number of points of lethal damage to bashing. The number of points downgraded is equal to 1 plus her Glory Renown.<br /> <br /> <b>FUSE BONE (HONOR)</b><br /> With this Facet the Uratha has learned to share her regenerative abilities with her packmates.<br /> <b>Cost: </b>1 Essence<br /> <b>Action:</b> Instant<br /> By touching a packmate and using this Facet, the Uratha heals her packmate of a number of points of lethal damage equal to her Honor Renown. The Uratha may not benefit from her own regeneration on the same turn that she activates this Facet.<br /> <br /> <b>PRIMAL HEALING (PURITY)</b><br /> This Facet draws upon the ancestral power of <i>Urfarah</i>, enhancing the Werewolf\u2019s natural healing abilities.<br /> <b>Cost:</b> None<br /> <b>Duration:</b> Permanent<br /> Whenever the Uratha would heal due to regeneration, she regains one additional health point. The amount of bashing healed per turn increases by one, and she heals an additional point of lethal damage every fifteen minutes, and an additional point of aggravated damage every four days.<br /> <br /> <b>MOTHER\u2019S EMBRACE (WISDOM)</b><br /> Under her mother\u2019s caring gaze the Uratha finds her worst injuries are soothed.<br /> <b>Cost:</b> 2 Essence<br /> <b>Action:</b> Instant<br /> In place of the Uratha\u2019s usual regeneration, she instead downgrades a single point of aggravated damage to lethal. This Facet may only be used while the Uratha\u2019s Auspice Moon is in the sky.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-26T14:22:39-07:00","id":1461612,"title":"","content":"I would rephrase Feign Weakness to grant as many bonus dice as you would have healed. So if you can regenerate 3, but you're at full health, it does nothing. If you're only down two health, you can only get +2 dice.<br /> <br /> It's not a huge deal, but it feels more elegant to me and more natural that a minor cut doesn't allow powerful Uratha to feint as easily as a having a giant chunk of flesh torn out of their torso.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-26T14:46:18-07:00","id":1461617,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1461016-reworking-werewolf-regeneration?p=1461612#post1461612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would rephrase Feign Weakness to grant as many bonus dice as you would have healed. So if you can regenerate 3, but you're at full health, it does nothing. If you're only down two health, you can only get +2 dice.<br /> <br /> It's not a huge deal, but it feels more elegant to me and more natural that a minor cut doesn't allow powerful Uratha to feint as easily as a having a giant chunk of flesh torn out of their torso.</div> </div> </div> </div>That\u2019s kind of the idea, but I couldn\u2019t find a way to phrase it clearly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-27T07:45:25-07:00","id":1461683,"title":"","content":"I like the gift but I feel like it needs a bit of work still, but to be honest it's a gift that makes way too much sense for werewolves if you think about it.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Maybe add a restriction for Silver in there? it is suppose to be the bane of werewolves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24813-aeveth","name":"Aeveth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24813_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"86"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-27T09:53:03-07:00","id":1461705,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aeveth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1461016-reworking-werewolf-regeneration?p=1461683#post1461683\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like the gift but I feel like it needs a bit of work still, but to be honest it's a gift that makes way too much sense for werewolves if you think about it.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Maybe add a restriction for Silver in there? it is suppose to be the bane of werewolves.</div> </div> </div> </div>Werewolf Merits have a nice feature where they often come with a drawback. I don't think any Gifts have drawbacks but that could be a nice feature. I don't think each Facet should have its own drawback, just one drawback that is activated when you buy any Facet.<br /> <br /> I like the Silver idea but not sure what it should be. The obvious one is to make silver more deadly, but the Pure already have an increased vulnerability to silver and I don't want to overlap with that. Maybe Silver could cause Death Rage more easily or something along those lines?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-27T11:52:48-07:00","id":1461736,"title":"","content":"When they said a restriction I think it was meant as in the gift not working properly on silver damage. Silver-inflicted damage could be resistant to the effect, or cause an extra loss of essence when the Gift is used.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-27T13:34:57-07:00","id":1461759,"title":"","content":"I'm not really sure why you'd need to add silver penalties to this. Only 2/5 facets are going to matter to silver damage anyway via dealing with agg damage; and they're already not going to help you much in combat against silver weapons. One is a downtime regen boost, which means you got away from the silver weapon using enemies and it just shortens how long you need to keep your head down to avoid having to fight them again before you're fully healed (or if you killed them how long you need to rest before the next threat comes around). The other is already pretty heavily restricted costing 2 Essence and only working during your Auspice moon phase to replace your normal regen with downgrading agg to lethal. It's not cost effective or fast enough to actually stand up to anything that can keep attacking you for agg damage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-04T18:46:57-07:00","id":1462749,"title":"","content":"Not saying to add penalties, but like a quirk of the whole gift since Silver is the werewolves bane, none of those facets would work on silver wounds, no downgrading, not healing a packmate (facet doesn't specify if the packmate is a werewolf)<br /> <br /> Not sure if it would be too strong, but I would suggest one of the effects being forcing a clash of wills rolls to take aggravated from a supernatural source, otherwise the damage is downgraded to lethal instead, could be a better version of either the Purity or Wisdom facets","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24813-aeveth","name":"Aeveth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24813_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"86"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}